    <h2>The locks, and why order matters<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Print Shop is not a room with a puzzle in it; it is a chain of dependencies with a shop painted on top. Once you see it that way, the level stops being a hunt and becomes a checklist, and the checklist has an order because each step consumes the output of the one before it — with exactly one optional link in the middle.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>The jerseys feed the till.</b> Four numbers on the wall — 3, 13, 9, 51 — become the six-digit till code <b>133951</b>. Nothing else in the room produces those digits, and no other lock accepts letters or names, so the jerseys have exactly one consumer.</li>
      <li><b>The till feeds the CD.</b> There is no other way into the cash drawer. The drawer is shut until the code lands, and the CD inside it is the only disc in the level.</li>
      <li><b>The CD feeds the computer — in one of our runs.</b> In the 4:44 recording the disc goes into the tower's bay and the monitor answers with the struck-through printer and the print-job warning. In the long run the CD stays in the bag and the wheel appears anyway once the printer has ink, so we are not going to pretend this link is mandatory. It is the difference between knowing why the print job is blocked and simply unblocking it.</li>
      <li><b>The cartridge feeds the printer.</b> The floor copier will not move until it has ink, and while it is dry the computer refuses to print. This is the one step that is easy to do out of order, because the cartridge is found in a cabinet on the opposite side of the room from both machines.</li>
      <li><b>The wheel feeds the printout.</b> 54 on the monitor, PRINT, PRINTING on the panel, and the sheet lands on the printer's tray and goes into the bag.</li>
      <li><b>The printout feeds the door.</b> This is the part most guides get wrong. The Printed picture is not a souvenir of the wheel puzzle — it is the door's code list, six numbers laid out on one sheet, and the keypad wants one of them. Which one is not fixed: our three runs opened with <b>8</b>, <b>18</b> and <b>24</b>, and 8 was refused outright in the run that opened with 24.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The practical consequence is that most "dead ends" in this level are steps attempted too early. The printer saying it is missing something is the cartridge step, not a separate puzzle; the wheel screen refusing to advance is the missing ink; the door rejecting a number is simply the wrong one of six. If you are stuck for more than five minutes in the Print Shop, the useful question is rarely "what is the code" — it is "which object is still sitting in my bag unused".</p>
    <p>There is a second, subtler structure worth noticing: the level hands you the numbers before it hands you the locks. The jerseys are readable from the first second of the run, and the wheel is fully visible from 5:10 — nearly three minutes before the recording types 54. This is a game that expects you to write things down, and the ending makes that literal: the door code is a number you are holding on a piece of paper. Anyone playing without a notepad ends up walking the floor twice, and the route between the till, the computer and the copier is the longest walk in the shop.</p>

    
<h2>The wheel: how 8, 13, 18, 24, 39 give you 54<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The wheel is the only genuine puzzle in the Print Shop, and it is gentler than it looks. Six slices, five numbers, one question mark, laid out so that each number has a partner directly across the circle. Read the pairs rather than the sequence and the rule falls out immediately:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>8</b> sits opposite <b>24</b>, and 8 × 3 = 24.</li>
      <li><b>13</b> sits opposite <b>39</b>, and 13 × 3 = 39.</li>
      <li><b>18</b> sits opposite the question mark, so the blank is 18 × 3 = <b>54</b>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Two independent pairs is enough to fix a single rule, and the third pair confirms it: this is not a sequence puzzle where you have to guess whether the step is +5 or +6, it is a pairing puzzle where the same operation has to hold twice before you commit. That is also why the numbers look scrambled going round the circle — 8, 13, 18, 24, 39 is not an arithmetic series in order, and players who try to read it clockwise as one sequence spend a long time on differences that do not matter.</p>
    <p>The answer is confirmed twice over in our footage. In the full run the value 54 is typed at 8:02 and the copier reports PRINTING at 8:36; in the second recording the same 54 goes in and the same PRINTING follows within seconds. The sheet afterwards does double duty: it shows the whole wheel with the blank filled in, so you can check your own answer, and it is the list you take to the door. One puzzle, two uses, which is a tidier bit of design than it first looks.</p>
    <p>One detail that trips people up: the keypad on the monitor is not a phone pad. It is two rows of five, <b>1 2 3 4 5</b> on top and <b>6 7 8 9 0</b> underneath, so the digits of 54 are not where your thumb expects them. The input line sits between the keypad and the PRINT button, and the X beside it clears the field — useful, because there is no backspace on this pad and a mistyped first digit otherwise means cancelling out of the whole screen and starting again.</p>

    <h2>The exit door: the sheet is the code list<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The way out is the grey-framed door on the back wall with the tall blue glass panel. It carries a <b>3×3 keypad</b> with an LCD on the frame — 1 to 9 in three rows, then <b>X</b>, <b>0</b> and a return arrow — and a <b>keyhole</b> in an oval plate directly underneath. The keypad is the lock that ends the level, and the clue for it is the thing you just printed.</p>
    <p>Here is what three different players typed, and what happened. In the full run: <b>54</b> at 9:58, which clears the display and does nothing; <b>39</b> at 10:17, which turns the LCD red and clears it; then <b>8</b> at 10:23, which opens the door at 10:24. In the 2:55 recording: <b>54</b> at 2:29, red and cleared; <b>8</b> at 2:37, which flashes green; then <b>18</b> at 2:44, which opens the door. In the 4:44 recording: <b>8</b> at 4:20, red and cleared; <b>392</b> at 4:32, wrong and cleared; then <b>24</b> at 4:35, which opens the door.</p>
    <p>Three runs, three different accepted values — <b>8</b>, <b>18</b>, <b>24</b> — and every one of them is one of the six numbers printed on the sheet in your bag. The rejected entries are instructive too: 54 is refused in the full run <i>and</i> in the 2:55 run, 39 is refused in the full run, and 8 is refused in the 4:44 run but accepted in the full run. A code that is right in one recording and wrong in another is not a code, it is a random draw. The door picks one of the six slices and you have to find which.</p>
    <p>So the ending is a short brute force, and the sheet is the list to brute force from. Type <b>8</b>, then <b>13</b>, then <b>18</b>, then <b>24</b>, then <b>39</b>, then <b>54</b>, clearing with X between attempts, until the door swings. The panel tells you which attempt failed: a rejected number turns the LCD red, and the run that opened on 18 saw 8 flash green on the way in, so the display is not purely binary. The door opening is unmissable either way, because the camera drops straight back to the shop floor. In our three runs it never took more than three attempts, but that is luck rather than a rule: any of the six can be the answer on a given playthrough.</p>
    <p>One more thing worth knowing before you get here: the level will not open the door until the <b>Printed picture</b> is actually in your inventory, not merely sitting on the printer's output tray. In the full run the sheet is collected at 9:26 and the door is opened at 10:24; in the 2:55 run the sheet is collected at 2:16 and the door opens at 2:45. If you have tried all six numbers and nothing works, check the bag first — you may be typing at a door that has not been armed yet.</p>
    <p>The keyhole is still a loose end. There is an oval lock plate under the keypad in every run, and in none of them is there a key in it or a key picked up anywhere on camera — the level finishes on the keypad number alone. Whatever the cylinder is for, it is not part of the solution we recorded.</p>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The till code order.</b> The wall reads HARRY 3, NERO 13, JACOB 9, DANNY 51, so the natural entry is 313951 — and it is rejected. The working code is <b>133951</b>, which leads with NERO's 13. This page cannot explain why 13 comes first: alphabetical order by name (DANNY, HARRY, JACOB, NERO) gives 513913, shirt-colour order gives the wall order, ascending gives 391351 and descending gives 511393, and none of them is 133951. Treat the code as the answer and the derivation as the level's one piece of unexplained design.</li>
      <li><b>Typing digits on a QWERTY.</b> The till's overlay is a computer keyboard, not a number pad. The digits live on the top row, and the number row is small on a phone screen. Two of the three recordings fumble here — one of them types 313951 first and has to clear the field.</li>
      <li><b>Using the CD on the printer.</b> The disc belongs in the desktop tower, not the copier, and the two machines sit on opposite sides of the room with drive-like slots on both. Our long run never uses the CD at all and still finishes, so if the monitor stays black when you Use it, you have not broken anything — go and put ink in the printer instead.</li>
      <li><b>Ignoring the cabinet.</b> The first ink cartridge is in the low cabinet under the mug shelves, which reads as display furniture rather than storage. Players who skip it reach the printer, get the "missing something" banner, and go looking for a code instead of a consumable.</li>
      <li><b>Treating the wheel as a sequence.</b> Read clockwise, 8, 13, 18, 24, 39 looks like it is building towards something and then breaks at the jump to 39. It is not a series; it is three pairs across the circle. Count the differences and you will be there for the full three minutes the reference run spends on this screen.</li>
      <li><b>Typing one door number and quitting.</b> The keypad on the exit door is where most of the frustration in this level lives, and it is not because the code is hard. It is because there are six candidates and no pointer. All three of our recordings type at least one number that turns the LCD red. If the door does not move, try the next number from the sheet — do not go looking for a seventh clue, because there is not one.</li>
      <li><b>Assembling the six numbers into one long code.</b> A very natural mistake given that the till code is a concatenation: type 81318243954, or 8-13-18-24-39-54 in some order, and watch it fail. The door wants a single number from the sheet, not the sheet as a sequence. In our footage the accepted entries are 8, 18 and 24 — one, two and three digits respectively.</li>
      <li><b>Arriving at the door without the sheet.</b> The keypad is dead until the Printed picture is in your inventory. It is not enough that the printer has finished; you have to open the tray output and take it. If every number fails, that is the first thing to check.</li>
      <li><b>Assuming the mugs matter.</b> Six mugs with coloured handles on the back-left shelves, in the same colour families as the jerseys, sitting directly above the cabinet that holds a cartridge — they look like a colour-order puzzle and they are not. Our run spends nearly a minute tapping around that shelf, and nothing there changes state.</li>
    </ul>

    
