    <div class="warn">
      <h2>What this page could not verify</h2>
      <p><b>1. How the exit door picks its number.</b> The keypad accepts one number from the printed sheet, and it is a different one in each recording: <b>8</b> in the full run, <b>18</b> in the 2:55 run, <b>24</b> in the 4:44 run — with 8 refused outright in the run that took 24. That is enough to rule out a fixed code, but not enough to say whether the game draws at random, keys the answer to something in the room we have not identified, or varies by build. What we can say is that all three accepted values appear on the sheet, that a rejected entry turns the LCD red, and that six attempts cover every candidate we have ever seen accepted.</p>
      <p><b>2. Why the till code starts with 13.</b> The digits are the four jersey numbers and the accepted order is 13 · 3 · 9 · 51, but no ordering rule we tested reproduces it. The code itself is solid — read twice, in two different videos — but the logic behind the order is not something this page can explain.</p>
      <p><b>3. How many ink cartridges the printer wants.</b> We see four separate cartridge pickups in the full run (1:39, 2:10, 4:04 and 4:39) and two in the 4:44 recording, and the printer's panel has a full row of colour swatches rather than one slot. Whether the machine needs one cartridge or several before it wakes up, we could not confirm: in every run the panel lights up on the first Use we can see clearly, and the inventory count is ambiguous because it doubles as the bag's slot counter.</p>
      <p><b>4. The keyhole.</b> Every recording shows an oval keyhole plate under the exit keypad; none shows a key in it, and none shows a key being picked up. The level finishes on the keypad number alone in all three runs. What the cylinder is for remains unexplained — it may be that the game simply reuses a door prop that normally takes a key.</p>
      <p><b>5. One reward panel we cannot reconcile.</b> Two recordings end on the same results screen — <b>+50</b> coins, nine of ten efficiency segments, <b>Time: 9:52</b> in the full run. A third, 2:55 recording of the same shop, clearly made on a different build or account, ends on a panel with an efficiency figure of <b>+5</b>, no coin reward and a piggy-bank prompt. We could not establish what separates the two, so the page quotes the +50 that two independent runs agree on and treats the 2:55 panel as an outlier.</p>
      <p><b>6. The mug shelf.</b> Six mugs, six handle colours, a cabinet underneath holding a cartridge. Our long run spends 48 seconds on that shelf and the frames show no reliable state change — brightness shifts on individual mugs are consistent with a hover highlight following the finger, not with a solved puzzle. If the mugs are a puzzle, we could not read its rule.</p>
      <p><b>7. Whether the CD is required at all.</b> The 4:44 recording puts the CD in the desktop tower and gets the print-job warning; our long run never uses it and reaches the same wheel and the same printout. Two recordings, two behaviours, and no third run that settles it — so the page presents the CD as the safe move and not as a gate.</p>
      <p>Everything else on this page — the four jersey numbers, <b>133951</b> on the till display, the CD in the open drawer, both warning lines, the six wheel numbers, <b>54</b>, <b>PRINTING</b>, the six numbers on the printed sheet, <b>+50</b> and <b>9:52</b> — was read off the screen at least twice, from at least two different recordings where the number mattered.</p>
    </div>
